From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:41:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Add transformed drop and inset shadows

Box shadows could only be drawn for axis-aligned elements: the Shadow
primitive's blurred rounded rect was evaluated in screen space. A canvas
showing rotated cards (sticky notes with CSS-like shadows) had no way to
cast an exact shadow under any rotation.

Shadow gets a `transformation` (like MonochromeSprite and
PolychromeSprite), and Window::paint_drop_shadows_transformed and
Window::paint_inset_shadows_transformed take one in scaled pixels, as
paint_image_transformed does (paint_drop_shadows and paint_inset_shadows
are the identity case). The element's bounds, the shadows' offsets, blur
and spread are in the element's own space; the vertex shaders transform
the shadow's quad (grown by three standard deviations for a drop shadow,
the element for an inset one) and pass the point in shadow space to the
fragment shaders, which evaluate the blur there. Hard edges (no blur, the
inset clip to the element) of a transformed shadow are anti-aliased by
their screen-space gradient, as transformed sprites are. Untransformed
shadows keep their snapped bounds and screen-space evaluation.

The scene culls and orders a transformed shadow by the bounding box of its
transformed quad (Primitive::painted_bounds), blur included.

Window::paint_outer_shadows_transformed draws drop shadows as CSS draws an
outer box-shadow: clipped inside the element's border box (Shadow's
`inset` field gets a third value, 2), so a translucent element does not
show its own shadow through it. paint_drop_shadows keeps GPUI's unclipped
drop shadows.

Window::with_shadow_clip clips the shadows painted inside it to a turned
rectangle (ShadowClip: scaled px -> the rectangle's own space and its
size), anti-aliased in the fragment shader, as a rotated container clips
its children's box shadows; content masks can only be axis-aligned.

Metal, WGSL (validated by the gpui_wgpu naga tests) and HLSL
(unvalidated) shaders are updated alike; the WebGL instance decoder reads
the fourteen extra words.
